A small store built on useSyncExternalStore. No provider needed; the hook is the store.
import { create } from "zustand";
import { useShallow } from "zustand/react/shallow";
export const useEditor = create((set, get) => ({
selectedIds: [],
zoom: 1,
select: id => set(s => ({ selectedIds: [...s.selectedIds, id] })),
clearSelection: () => set({ selectedIds: [] }),
zoomIn: () => set({ zoom: Math.min(get().zoom * 1.25, 8) }),
}));
// Component: renders only when zoom changes
const zoom = useEditor(s => s.zoom);
// Selecting several fields: use useShallow, or the selector returns a new object each time
const { zoom, zoomIn } = useEditor(useShallow(s => ({ zoom: s.zoom, zoomIn: s.zoomIn })));
In Zustand v5, a selector that returns a new object on every call without useShallow can cause an infinite render loop — the same snapshot rule as Chapter 3.

The official, modern way to write Redux: createSlice (with Immer for "mutating" syntax), configureStore (good defaults, DevTools, a check for accidental mutation in development), and RTK Query for server state.
import { createSlice, configureStore } from "@reduxjs/toolkit";
const editorSlice = createSlice({
name: "editor",
initialState: { selectedIds: [], zoom: 1 },
reducers: {
selected(state, action) { state.selectedIds.push(action.payload); }, // Immer makes this immutable
selectionCleared(state) { state.selectedIds = []; },
zoomedIn(state) { state.zoom = Math.min(state.zoom * 1.25, 8); },
},
});
export const { selected, selectionCleared, zoomedIn } = editorSlice.actions;
export const store = configureStore({ reducer: { editor: editorSlice.reducer } });
// Component
const zoom = useSelector(state => state.editor.zoom);
const dispatch = useDispatch();

Why does this crash when user changes from null to an object? Fix it.
function Greeting({ user }) {
if (!user) return <p>Please sign in</p>;
const [wave, setWave] = useState(false);
return <p onClick={() => setWave(true)}>Hi {user.name}{wave && "!"}</p>;
}
Solution
The first render calls zero hooks, the next calls one; React reads the hook list by position and detects the mismatch. Move the hook above the early return (or split into two components: Greeting returns <SignedInGreeting user={user} />).

The display sticks at 1. Why? Fix it without adding count to the dependencies.
const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
useEffect(() => {
const id = setInterval(() => setCount(count + 1), 1000);
return () => clearInterval(id);
}, []);
Solution
The interval callback closed over count from the first render, which is always 0, so it sets 1 forever. Use the updater form, setCount(c => c + 1): the effect no longer reads count, the empty dependency list is now honest, and the interval never restarts.
